hey this is presh talwalkar  the holderness family posted this math  problem for first grade students to  their facebook page  it is causing confusion and currently  being covered by major news outlets  in this video i want to explain what  many people believe to be the correct  answer  let me first read the problem  the picture shows some letters that are  arranged in a pattern  use the key to find the missing letter  the key is a conversion from letters to  numerical values  a is equal to 5  b is equal to 6 c is equal to 7 and so  on where each letter is equal to the  next number going all the way to z  equaling 30.  the picture is a three by three grid  that only involves the four corner  squares and the center square in the  first row  the two corner squares are question mark  and g  the second row is the center square b  and the third row is the corner squares  s and p  what is the value of the question mark  give it a try and now let me explain  what many people believe to be the  correct answer  we'll first convert the letters to  numerical values  g is equal to 11 b is equal to 6  s is equal to 23  and p is equal to 20.  what could the pattern be  many people see the pattern that the  numbers in the diagonal entries  should have the same numerical sum  this would lead to the equation 11 plus  6 plus 23 would be equal to question  mark plus 6 plus 20.  this means that 40 would be equal to  question mark plus 26 and therefore  question mark would be equal to 14  and that converts back  into the alphabetical value of j  so according to this pattern  the question mark should be equal to j  this is the main pattern that i saw and  many people saw  do you see any other patterns what do  you think the answer should be  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mindyourdecisions which you can  follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at presh